      "rationale": "Inverted prior: searched for an end-to-end solution that lets sustained-strategy or time-varying TTE pipelines take probabilistic (non-thresholded) treatment, confounder, and outcome phenotypes and propagate differential misclassification through weights and censoring into the regime-effect interval. Nothing retrieved closes that full claim.\n\nOwn citations first. Wang et al. (npj Digit Med 2026; 10.1038/s41746-026-02563-z) is an operational TTE framework paper. Available text describes validation samples and probabilistic or uncertainty-aware outcome models as tools that can account for measurement error, and separately reviews g-methods for sustained strategies. It does not deliver a validated pipeline that accepts probabilistic treatment and outcome nodes end to end, nor simulations of regime-effect bias under differential time-varying misclassification. Description of existing pieces is not resolution. Benz et al. (Am J Epidemiol 2025; 10.1093/aje/kwae471; arXiv:2406.16830) targets selection bias from missing eligibility criteria via IPW under an MAR-type assumption for eligibility ascertainment. It is a useful template for validation-anchored IPW on one protocol component; it is not phenotype measurement-error correction for time-varying exposure or confounders.\n\nComponent prior art (does not close the stated gap). Hubbard, Tong, Duan, Chen (Epidemiology 2020; 10.1097/EDE.0000000000001193) give a hand-computable bias correction for continuous EHR probabilistic outcome phenotypes in association studies. Scope is outcome misclassification and association parameters, not time-varying treatment weights or sustained regimes. Edwards et al. (Am J Epidemiol 2018; 10.1093/aje/kwy028) fold sensitivity analysis for cause-of-death misclassification into the parametric g-formula; that is quantitative bias analysis, not identification with validation-anchored probabilistic nodes. Edwards et al. (RIME; PubMed 31971584, 2020) reparameterize imputation for exposure misclassification with validation data and illustrate combination with IPW for hazard ratios and risks: point-exposure misclassification with IPW, not full longitudinal differential error in exposure, confounders, and outcomes under dynamic or sustained strategies. Nab et al. (Epidemiology 2020) quantify bias from a misclassified confounder in MSM-IPW; characterization and QBA, not a solved TTE pipeline. Lu et al. (J Biomed Inform 2024; 10.1016/j.jbi.2024.104690) leverage multiple error-prone algorithm phenotypes plus a small validation set for risk-factor associations: again association, not TTE g-methods.\n\nMaterial that strengthens openness. Ross, Cole, et al. (Epidemiology 2024; 35(2):196–207) document challenges of transporting measurement-error parameters (sensitivity, specificity, and related bias parameters) from external validation data: this supports the report’s claim that Se/Sp transport across sites is itself an untested or fragile transport claim, not a solved step.\n\nSoftware (CRAN/GitHub scan). TrialEmulation, gfoRmula, gfoRmulaICE, lmtp, ltmle, survtmle, WeightIt, MatchIt, cobalt, ipw, adjustedCurves, riskRegression, and related Python stacks (zEpid, causallib, lifelines, scikit-survival) remain binary or continuous node pipelines for treatment and outcome after analyst thresholding. CRAN’s causal-inference task view points measurement-error tooling to sensitivity packages (e.g., episensr, EValue), not first-class probabilistic phenotype inputs inside sequential TTE or g-formula estimation. No merged PR or release note retrieved that closes end-to-end phenotype uncertainty in these packages through 2025–2026.\n\nQueries run (representative): Wang operational TTE DOI and Nature page/PDF; Benz kwae471/arXiv; “probabilistic phenotype” + causal/IPTW/g-formula/lmtp/ltmle/TTE 2023–2026; measurement error misclassification + MSM-IPW / g-formula / time-varying / sustained strategy; Hubbard probabilistic phenotypes; Edwards RIME and g-formula misclassification; Ross transporting measurement error; CRAN/GitHub TrialEmulation gfoRmula lmtp measurement error; TARGET/ENCePP/FDA RWE measurement error guidance cross-check. Journals and preprint venues searched via web for 2024–2026 hits on those terms.\n\nCaveat on support: the entry is faithful that standard TTE pipelines threshold phenotypes and that the nonlinear interaction of differential time-varying misclassification with weighting and censoring is not handled end to end. It would be overstated only if read as “no methods exist for any piece of phenotype error.” Pieces exist (outcome probabilistic correction in associations; exposure misclassification with IPW; g-formula outcome SA; transport problems formalized). None of those pieces, alone or as packaged software, solves the problem as stated for sustained-strategy emulation with uncertainty carried to the final interval.",
      "weakest_true_restatement": null,
